      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(signal): retry inbound flushes on reply session init conflict",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes #100944. On the Signal channel, a follow-up DM sent shortly after a prior turn completes can be silently dropped when it hits `reply session initialization conflicted`. The Signal debounce `onFlush`/`onError` path only logged the error and did not retry, unlike Slack (#99647) and Telegram which already special-case this exact error for bounded retry-with-backoff.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe Slack and Telegram channel handlers recognize that a reply-session-init conflict is a transient race (the previous turn is still releasing its reply session) and retry the inbound flush a bounded number of times with exponential backoff. Signal was missing this behavior, so valid user messages were lost with no user-visible feedback.\n\nThe change ports the same retry semantics to Signal:\n- Detects `reply session initialization conflicted for <sessionKey>` anywhere in the error graph (matches Slack/Telegram/WhatsApp regex).\n- Re-enqueues the original debounced entries with `retryAttempt` incremented.\n- Uses exponential backoff delays of 1s / 2s / 4s, capped at 3 attempts.\n- Non-conflict flush failures and exhausted retries continue to propagate/log as before.\n\n## User Impact\n\nSignal DMs sent immediately after a bot reply are now retried instead of silently dropped. No configuration change is required.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Added `extensions/signal/src/monitor/event-handler.reply-session-conflict.test.ts` with 4 tests covering:\n  1. Retry succeeds on the second attempt after a conflict.\n  2. Retry gives up after 3 attempts and logs the failure.\n  3. Non-conflict errors are not retried.\n  4. Batched debounced entries are retried independently.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/signal/src/monitor/event-handler.reply-session-conflict.test.ts --run` → 4/4 passed.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/signal/src/monitor/event-handler.*.test.ts --run` → 69/69 passed (no regressions in existing Signal event-handler tests).\n- `npx oxfmt --check ...` and `node scripts/run-oxlint.mjs ...` → clean.\n- `pnpm tsgo:extensions` and `pnpm tsgo:extensions:test` → clean.\n",
        "branch": "fix/signal-reply-session-conflict-retry",
